extend templates/layout.pug

block navigation
    div

block content
    .container
        h1 Playground

        p.flow-text Paste a descriptor to see what it takes to spend from it. A group exports its wallet as a standard descriptor; pick #[strong Bitcoin] as the network, start the wallet to sync its balance and addresses, or open it in the policy compiler to see its spending conditions as blocks.

        p This is the #[a(href="https://bitcoindevkit.org/bdk-cli/playground/", target="_blank") bdk-cli playground] from the Bitcoin Dev Kit, running in your browser. It only ever needs public keys: never paste a private key or seed into it.

        p The policy compiler runs entirely on this page and sends nothing anywhere. #[strong Starting the wallet does not.] Syncing asks #[a(href="https://blockstream.info", target="_blank") blockstream.info] for the history of every address the descriptor derives, which tells them the whole of the wallet and that someone here is interested in it. Compile freely; sync only a descriptor you are willing to hand over.

        h2 The structure

        p Build the shape of a group's wallets — the group, the groups under it, the wallets it guards for its members — and see what the chain is asked to enforce for any one of them.

        #wallet-simulator.simulator

        p.simulator-caveats
            strong What this does not model.
            |  A policy that compiles is not a group that works, and a tree that draws is not the whole of one address. Seven of Bennu's rules are nowhere in what you see above, and each is kept somewhere else:
        ul.simulator-caveats-list
            li
                strong The ceremony.
                |  The tree says 3 of 5 sign. It does not say that #[em every] member has to take part to make the key in the first place, which is the step a group actually gets stuck on.
            li
                strong The birth certificate.
                |  A subgroup's parentage is a statement its parent's quorum signed, not a script. Nothing on-chain checks it; every honest client does.
            li
                strong The derivation.
                |  Real addresses come off BIP86's unhardened tail under a public account step tweaked from the group's key. The simulator names a key and stops there.
            li
                strong The cap.
                |  Bennu will not propose more than 100,000 sats in one spend on mainnet. That is a gate in the app, and the chain would happily take more.
            li
                strong One address.
                |  The internal key is #[code account(K, network, n)/change/index], so #[em every address has its own tree], with its own leaves and its own root. The drawing shows one of them.
            li
                strong The leaf keys.
                |  A guardian's key is derived one step further per child, so the same group's leaf is a different key in every descendant's tree. That is what makes a revealed leaf unlinkable to a group.
            li
                strong The key path.
                |  #[code P] is a FROST threshold key that several members have to co-operate to use. The taproot tree can no more show that than the policy can — it is one key either way.

        h2 The policy

        //- An iframe keeps materialize's styles off the playground's own inputs.
        iframe(src="/static/playground/playground.html", title="bdk-cli playground", style="width: 100%; height: 1200px; border: none;")

block additionalScripts
